exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

The trigger has to decide which column the writer touched. On `INSERT`
there is no `OLD`, so the rule is simpler: whichever of the two is
non-NULL wins. On `UPDATE`, compare each column to its old value to see
which one changed.

## Hint 2

```sql
CREATE FUNCTION sync_note() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
  IF TG_OP = 'INSERT' THEN
    IF NEW.note IS NULL THEN NEW.note := NEW.memo;
    ELSE NEW.memo := NEW.note;
    END IF;
  ELSE
    IF NEW.note IS DISTINCT FROM OLD.note THEN NEW.memo := NEW.note;
    ELSE NEW.note := NEW.memo;
    END IF;
  END IF;
  RETURN NEW;
END $$;
```

A `BEFORE` row trigger must return `NEW` — returning `NULL` silently
cancels the write.

## Hint 3

Decide what happens when a writer sets *both* columns to different
values, because your trigger will encounter it the day two deploys
overlap. The version above lets `note` win on update and `memo` win on
insert-when-note-is-null, which is arbitrary but deterministic.
Deterministic is the requirement; which one wins matters less than that
the answer is the same every time.

